WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:10.310
A merchant's settlement currency, EUR, sits in a box that holds anything: a box of Any, or Object in Java.

2
00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:11.961
One function writes it.

3
00:00:11.961 --> 00:00:15.264
Another team's function reads it, hours later.

4
00:00:15.467 --> 00:00:22.663
One day, the writer stores 978, the numeric code for the euro, instead of the letters EUR.

5
00:00:22.867 --> 00:00:25.630
The compiler is happy: an Int is an Any.

6
00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:28.256
The writer returns, and nothing fails.

7
00:00:28.467 --> 00:00:32.143
Later, the reader takes the value out, and casts it to a String.

8
00:00:32.367 --> 00:00:35.744
Where does the program fail: at the write, or at the read?

9
00:00:37.533 --> 00:00:38.387
At the read.

10
00:00:38.387 --> 00:00:42.368
The cast throws a ClassCastException, inside the reader.

11
00:00:42.567 --> 00:00:44.498
The stack trace names the reader.

12
00:00:44.498 --> 00:00:47.423
The writer, where the bug is, is not in it at all.

13
00:00:47.423 --> 00:00:51.227
The type check still happened, just late, and in the wrong place.

14
00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:54.326
Now write the type on the label.

15
00:00:54.326 --> 00:00:56.985
This box holds a String, and nothing else.

16
00:00:57.167 --> 00:00:59.206
The writer tries 978 again.

17
00:00:59.206 --> 00:01:04.945
This time the compiler rejects that very line, before the program ever runs.

18
00:01:05.167 --> 00:01:07.840
The error sits at the write, where the bug is.

19
00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:11.153
And the reader needs no cast: what comes out is a String.

20
00:01:12.067 --> 00:01:14.858
You don't want a new box class for every type.

21
00:01:14.858 --> 00:01:18.437
So write the box once, with a blank on its label, called T.

22
00:01:18.633 --> 00:01:20.517
Each use fills in the blank.

23
00:01:20.517 --> 00:01:22.602
A Box&lt;String&gt; for the currency.

24
00:01:22.602 --> 00:01:24.889
A Box&lt;Long&gt; for a limit of 50,000.

25
00:01:24.889 --> 00:01:26.840
A Box&lt;Payment&gt; for a payment.

26
00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:29.867
The compiler checks each one as its own type.

27
00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:35.290
How does the compiler check a call on limit?

28
00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:36.784
It fills in the blank.

29
00:01:36.784 --> 00:01:40.725
Limit is a Box&lt;Long&gt;, so every T inside Box reads as Long.

30
00:01:40.933 --> 00:01:45.075
Now someone calls replace on limit, with the String USD.

31
00:01:45.075 --> 00:01:46.259
Does it compile?

32
00:01:48.033 --> 00:01:48.249
No.

33
00:01:48.249 --> 00:01:52.633
For this box, replace takes a Long, and a String doesn't fit.

34
00:01:52.833 --> 00:01:55.524
The blank, T, is called a type parameter.

35
00:01:55.524 --> 00:01:59.396
The real type that fills it, here Long, is a type argument.

36
00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:03.183
In code, the blank goes in angle brackets.

37
00:02:03.183 --> 00:02:08.950
Kotlin works out the type argument from the value, and Java's diamond does the same.

38
00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:10.667
Both reject the USD line.

39
00:02:11.467 --> 00:02:18.833
The rule: write the type on the label once, and the compiler checks every value where it goes in, not where it comes out.

40
00:02:19.067 --> 00:02:20.084
A quick check.

41
00:02:20.084 --> 00:02:22.992
A Box&lt;Payment&gt; is handed the String EUR.

42
00:02:22.992 --> 00:02:26.191
Compile error, or a crash later at the read?
